content:
{
  "asset": "Pump P-102, AquaDyne HF-300, serial HF3-19870, filtered-water line",
  "priority": "P2",
  "stop_equipment_now": false,
  "condition_assessment": [
    {
      "statement": "Mechanical-seal leakage is a steady drip of approximately one drop per second. No numerical allowable leak-rate limit is documented, so a numerical pass/fail comparison cannot be made. The applicable documented criterion is the troubleshooting symptom 'Leakage at shaft seal'; its corrective action is to replace the mechanical seal and check alignment.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Drive-end housing vibration is 2.1 mm/s. The applicable limits are Figure 3's bearing-housing vibration-velocity zones, expressed in mm/s RMS: 2.1 is within Zone A (0.0-2.8, 'New machines') and below the 2.8 Zone A/B boundary. It is also well below Zone D (7.1-11.0, 'Damage likely - stop the pump'), so this reading does not trigger the documented vibration stop action. The report does not expressly state that the reading is RMS.",
      "citations": [
        "C07",
        "C06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Drive-end housing temperature is 66 C. The applicable bearing-housing temperature limits are normal up to 70 C, alarm at 80 C and trip at 90 C. Thus 66 C is within the normal range, 14 C below alarm and 24 C below trip. Bearing temperatures are measured on the housing surface.",
      "citations": [
        "C02",
        "C01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "The active seal leak requires planned corrective work, but the supplied vibration and temperature readings do not meet the documented stop or trip conditions. P2 is therefore a planning judgement for prompt seal replacement, not a classification derived from a supplied priority matrix; no immediate equipment stop is indicated by the documented limits.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C07",
        "C02"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "probable_causes": [
    {
      "statement": "Primary inspection hypothesis: worn or cracked mechanical-seal faces. This is the first probable cause listed for leakage at the shaft seal, although the leak alone does not confirm the seal-face condition.",
      "citations": [
        "C09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Second possibility to verify: the seal has run dry. The troubleshooting table lists this cause, while the specification requires Plan 11 flush from pump discharge and expressly prohibits dry running. No flush interruption is reported, so this remains unconfirmed.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Third possibility to verify: shaft misalignment. The troubleshooting table lists misalignment as a possible cause of seal leakage and directs an alignment check; the reported readings alone do not establish or exclude misalignment.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C10"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tasks": [
    {
      "statement": "Isolate and drain the pump before disassembly. Apply the site's chemical-exposure controls while draining and opening P-102 because this filtered-water line is dosed with sodium hypochlorite.",
      "citations": [
        "C10",
        "C16"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Remove the back pull-out assembly. Use lifting equipment because its mass is 68 kg. The back pull-out design allows removal of the rotating assembly without disturbing the casing or pipework.",
      "citations": [
        "C10",
        "C02",
        "C01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Remove the impeller nut and impeller, then withdraw the existing HF3-450 component seal and HF3-455 shaft sleeve.",
      "citations": [
        "C10",
        "C05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Inspect the removed seal faces for wear, cracking or evidence of dry running, and inspect the sleeve for scoring. Record the findings to distinguish the documented leakage causes. Do not automatically apply the service bulletin's 0.1 mm sleeve-scoring rejection threshold to this below-cutoff serial number.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C10",
        "C13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Fit one new HF3-450 component mechanical seal and one new HF3-120 casing gasket. Do not substitute HF3-450B: that substitution is mandated only for HF-300 pumps at serial HF3-22000 and above, whereas P-102 is HF3-19870.",
      "citations": [
        "C10",
        "C05",
        "C13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Reassemble and tighten disturbed fasteners to Table 5 with lightly oiled threads. Seal gland nuts M10: 25 Nm; impeller nut M24: 160 Nm. If disturbed, casing bolts M16: 210 Nm and bearing housing bolts M12: 85 Nm. The applicable seal-gland torque is 25 Nm, not the 30 Nm specified for HF3-450B on the higher serial range.",
      "citations": [
        "C11",
        "C10",
        "C13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "After reassembly, perform the alignment check required for shaft-seal leakage and use laser alignment where the work has moved the motor or bearing housing. Acceptance tolerances are parallel offset 0.05 mm and angular misalignment 0.05 mm per 100 mm.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Before returning the pump to service, restore the specified Plan 11 seal flush from pump discharge and ensure the pump is never run dry.",
      "citations": [
        "C02"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "parts": [
    {
      "part_number": "HF3-450",
      "description": "Mechanical seal, component type; required replacement for this serial range. HF3-450B is not applicable to serial HF3-19870.",
      "quantity": 1,
      "citations": [
        "C05",
        "C13",
        "C09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_number": "HF3-120",
      "description": "Casing gasket; install new during mechanical-seal replacement and whenever the casing is opened.",
      "quantity": 1,
      "citations": [
        "C05",
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_number": "HF3-455",
      "description": "Shaft sleeve, one installed; remove and inspect, but do not treat it as an automatic replacement. If scoring is found, obtain the applicable rejection criterion or engineering disposition for this serial before deciding whether to reuse or replace it.",
      "quantity": 1,
      "citations": [
        "C05",
        "C10",
        "C13"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "safety_controls": [
    {
      "statement": "Isolate and drain the pump before removing the back pull-out assembly or seal components.",
      "citations": [
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Use lifting equipment for the 68 kg back pull-out assembly; do not plan a manual lift.",
      "citations": [
        "C02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Control residual chemical exposure during draining and disassembly: P-102 falls within the P-101 to P-106 filtered-water pumps chemically dosed with sodium hypochlorite. The evidence does not specify the required PPE or decontamination method.",
      "citations": [
        "C16",
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Protect the replacement seal by using Plan 11 flush from pump discharge and never running the pump dry.",
      "citations": [
        "C02"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "labour": [
    {
      "statement": "Mechanical seal replacement: 4.0 hours with 2 technicians, using Table 6's standard labour time.",
      "citations": [
        "C12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Laser alignment: allow 1.5 hours with 1 technician when required by the alignment task and Section 5.4. Bearing-pair replacement labour is not included because neither bearing wear nor high bearing temperature is established by the report.",
      "citations": [
        "C12",
        "C10",
        "C02"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "source_conflicts": [
    {
      "statement": "Seal part number and gland torque conflict: the manual parts list and Table 5 specify HF3-450 with 25 Nm seal-gland torque, while Service Bulletin SB-2026-014, issued 14 March 2026, specifies HF3-450B and 30 Nm. The bulletin expressly applies to serial HF3-22000 and above and says pumps below that serial continue using HF3-450 and manual Table 5. P-102 is HF3-19870, so the manual configuration governs: install HF3-450 and use 25 Nm on lightly oiled M10 gland-nut threads; do not use HF3-450B or its 30 Nm torque.",
      "citations": [
        "C05",
        "C11",
        "C13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Sleeve-scoring criterion: SB-2026-014's instruction to replace HF3-455 when scoring exceeds 0.1 mm is within the instructions for pumps at serial HF3-22000 and above. It is not established as applicable to HF3-19870. For this asset, the manual requires sleeve inspection but supplies no numerical scoring limit, so the 0.1 mm threshold must not be silently carried over.",
      "citations": [
        "C13",
        "C10"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "open_questions": [
    "Is the reported 2.1 mm/s drive-end vibration an RMS velocity as required by Figure 3, and what is the non-drive-end housing reading? Section 3 requires weekly measurement on both housings and classification of the highest reading, so the supplied single reading is incomplete for that monitoring requirement (C06, C07).",
    "What numerical mechanical-seal leakage limit, if any, and what site priority/response-time matrix apply? The manual identifies shaft-seal leakage and its corrective action but gives no allowable drop rate, so the approximately one-drop-per-second rate cannot be compared with a numerical limit and P2 remains a planning judgement (C09).",
    "What sleeve-scoring rejection limit or engineering disposition applies to serial HF3-19870 if scoring is found? The manual only says to inspect the sleeve, and the bulletin's 0.1 mm criterion is scoped to serial HF3-22000 and above (C10, C13).",
    "What site-specific sodium-hypochlorite PPE, decontamination and drained-fluid handling controls are required before opening the pump? The evidence establishes chemical dosing but does not provide those controls (C16).",
    "Was Plan 11 seal flush continuously available, and is there any history of dry running? Inspection and operating history are needed to distinguish worn/cracked faces, dry running and misalignment rather than assuming a root cause (C02, C09)."
  ]
}
